.TH "DSCAN" "1" "Aug 13, 2026" "0.12" "mpiFileUtils"
.SH NAME
dscan \- distributed streaming directory scanner with histogram reporting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dscan
[\fIOPTION\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
\fBdscan\fR walks a directory tree in parallel using MPI and generates a JSON
report containing:
.sp
- file size histogram
- atime/mtime/ctime capacity histograms (bytes of regular files per age bucket)
- broken-path detection (exact total count, capped listing)
.sp
The walk is streaming: entries are folded into fixed-size accumulators as they
are discovered, statistics are combined with MPI reductions, and only up to
broken-limit records per rank travel to rank 0. Memory use per rank and
traffic to rank 0 are independent of the tree size, so very large trees
(billions of entries) can be scanned.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-directory PATH
Directory tree to scan. Required. Must be mounted at the same path on every
node running dscan ranks.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output FILE
Write JSON output to FILE. Required.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-print
Print a human-readable summary to stdout on rank 0.
.TP
.B \-b, \-\-batch-files N
Account scanning in batches of approximately N entries and print a progress
line on rank 0 as each batch completes.
.TP
.B \-\-broken-limit N
Maximum number of broken paths listed in the report. The total broken count is
always exact. Default is 100; 0 keeps only the count.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Enable verbose logging.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress verbose logging.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage.
.SH BROKEN-PATH RULES
.sp
An item is marked broken when one or more conditions hold, judged from the
lstat data collected during the walk and from actual scan errors (no extra
per-entry syscalls are issued):
.sp
- abnormal_size: regular file size is larger than 1 PiB
- missing: lstat failed with ENOENT/ENOTDIR during the scan
- abnormal_time: atime/mtime/ctime older than 10 years or in the future
- unreadable: a directory could not be opened or lstat failed otherwise
.SH EXAMPLE
.sp
\fBmpirun -np 8 dscan --directory /data --output report.json --batch-files 1000000 --print\fR
